Oersted's experiment shows that currents produce magnetic fields. The **Biot–Savart law** (Jean-Baptiste Biot and Félix Savart, 1820) gives the field produced by each small piece of a current, so that the field of any conductor can be found by adding the pieces.

Divide a conductor carrying a steady current $I$ into tiny vector elements $d\vec{l}$, each pointing in the direction of the current. The product $I\,d\vec{l}$ is called a **current element**. Let P be a point at position $\vec{r}$ measured from the element. The magnitude $dB$ of the field at P due to this element is:
- directly proportional to the current, $dB \propto I$;
- directly proportional to the length of the element, $dB \propto dl$;
- directly proportional to $\sin\theta$, where $\theta$ is the angle between $d\vec{l}$ and $\vec{r}$;
- inversely proportional to the square of the distance, $dB \propto 1/r^2$.

Combining these and inserting the SI constant gives the law.
